<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Telemark</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Telemark is a traditional region and county of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skagerrak">Skagerrak</a> coast to the mountain plateaus of the interior. Its inland districts were known for iron, whetstones, stave churches and rich folk traditions, and the region gave its name to telemark skiing. In 1943 the Norwegian resistance sabotaged the heavy-water plant at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vemork">Vemork</a> in Telemark.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/telemark.jpg" alt="Telemark" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjoertvedt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tinn_gaustatoppen_rjukan_IMG_0057.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Telemark lies in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, extending from the Hardangervidda mountain plateau in the north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skagerrak">Skagerrak</a> coast in the south. Its landscape includes a rugged coastline, valleys, lakes, hills and mountain plateaus. The name means the mark of the Thelir, the ancient North Germanic tribe that inhabited what is now Upper Telemark in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/migration-period">Migration Period</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>. The Norse form was Þelamǫrk, whose last element means woodland or borderland. Traditional Telemark, now called Upper Telemark, lies inland and makes up more than two thirds of the county by its traditional definition. The south, Grenland, is flatter, closer to the coast and more urban.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Upper Telemark had more self-owning farmers than any other region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and later. It kept Norse culture to a greater degree, in its more egalitarian society, religion, values and language, and the people were often described as the most violent in Norway. Its farmers held on to aspects of Old Norse religion and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> longer than other regions and often defied the central authorities of Denmark-Norway. Its dialects are the closest to Old Norse, and it has more buildings from medieval times than any other Norwegian region. Upper Telemark traditionally lacks cities entirely.</p><p>Grenland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skien">Skien</a> fjord were marked by cities, seafaring, trade and industry, with more contact with continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Skien was in the early modern period one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s most important cities, ahead of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Christiania</a>, though its importance declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. The playwright <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henrik-ibsen">Henrik Ibsen</a> was born there. During the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> the two regions became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Bratsberg, named after the farm where the governor had his seat. With the introduction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> in 1662 it became a county, and it was renamed Telemark in 1919.</p><p>Kviteseid in Upper Telemark is known as the birthplace of skiing as a modern sport. The Telemark style of skiing, invented by Sondre Norheim, and the Telemark landing of ski jumping are named after it. Telemark has also been a major industrial region for centuries, with paper mills in Grenland and heavy water and fertiliser production by Norsk Hydro in Upper Telemark.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Telemark merged with Vestfold in 2020, and on 1 January 2024 the county of Telemark was re-established. Its largest population centres are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skien">Skien</a>, Porsgrunn, Notodden, Rjukan and Kragerø. The international road E134, the fastest route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, runs through its inland municipalities. The Gea Norvegica Geopark, established in 2006 in the south, is the first UNESCO Global Geopark in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Telemark” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Grenland, around the lower Skienselva and the coast of modern <b>Telemark</b>, appears in the sagas as one of the small kingdoms of south-eastern Norway.</blockquote><a href="/state/grenland">Grenland</a></li></ul></section>
